@section main_subsystems Subsystems

Each subsystem with an architecture deep-dive links to its guide in the last column
(see @ref overviews and @ref deepdives).

| Subsystem    | Path         | Responsibility | Guide |
|--------------|--------------|----------------|-------|
| **HAL**      | `hal/`       | Unified platform interface (clocks, memory ordering, stack/PLC introspection, serial). `simulator.h` and `opta.h` implement it. | - |
| **Scheduler**| `scheduler/` | Non-blocking fixed-period loop driver with overrun handling; delegates CPU-load, jitter, and stack-usage metrics to `PerfMonitor`. | @ref md_docs_2SCHEDULER__PERF "Scheduler & Perf" |
| **Console** | `console/`  | @ref Botanik::Terminal::Console drives a multiplexed page display over the @ref Botanik::Terminal::AnsiTerminal VT device; @ref Botanik::Terminal::InputParser decodes byte streams into events; @ref Botanik::Terminal::TextEditor is a byte-efficient line editor. | @ref md_docs_2EVENT__DISPATCH "Event Dispatch", @ref md_docs_2EDITOR__RECONCILE "Editor Reconcile" |
| **Pages**    | `pages/`     | Full-screen views (`Shell`, `Logs`, `Home`, `Status`) behind a common @ref Botanik::Pages::Page interface. | @ref md_docs_2PAGES "Pages" |
| **Logging**  | `logs/`      | @ref Botanik::Logs::Logger rate-limits with a dual token-bucket, then fans entries out to @ref Botanik::Logs::LogWriter "LogWriter"s. | @ref md_docs_2LOG__FLOW "Log Flow" |
| **PLC I/O**  | `plcio/`     | @ref Botanik::PlcIO::PlcSyncHandler runs RX/TX state machines over shared memory with the PLC coprocessor; an emulator stands in for it on the simulator. | @ref md_docs_2PLC__SYNC "PLC Sync" |
| **Health**   | `health/`    | @ref Botanik::Health::HealthMonitor owns the alarm model and notifier, runs the power-on self-test, and gates the watchdog escalation. | @ref md_docs_2POST "Self-tests" |
| **Network**  | `network/`   | @ref Botanik::Network::NameResolver (non-blocking DNS), @ref Botanik::Network::NetworkHandler (device bring-up), @ref Botanik::Network::NtpClient (SNTP), and @ref Botanik::Network::MqttClient (MQTT publish) - PLC-config-driven via @ref Botanik::ConfigHandler. | @ref md_docs_2PLC__CONFIG "PLC config" |
| **Utils**    | `utils/`     | `BufferedSerial` (ring-buffered UART), `PerfMonitor` (loop CPU-load / jitter / stack high-water instrumentation), the `sun_tracker.h` solar-position math, and time/uptime helpers. | @ref md_docs_2SCHEDULER__PERF "Scheduler & Perf" |

@section main_features Optional features

Not every firmware wants every subsystem. `libbotanik` is useful to embedded
projects that have no PLC coprocessor at all - the console, logger, scheduler,
health/alarm model, and pages all stand on their own. Optional subsystems are
gated at compile time by `BOTANIK_FEATURE_*` flags in `src/libbotanik_features.h`,
which mirrors the `BOTANIK_*` config-override mechanism in `libbotanik_config.h`:
each flag defaults from an `#ifndef`-guarded macro, so a site pins it in
`sites/<name>/config.h` or the build sets it with `-DBOTANIK_FEATURE_<X>=ON/OFF`
(forwarded as a PUBLIC define on the `botanik` library, so the whole build agrees).

| Flag | Default | Gates | State |
|------|---------|-------|-------|
| `BOTANIK_FEATURE_PLC`     | **on**  | @ref Botanik::PlcIO::PlcSyncHandler, `PlcLogReader`, the shared-memory payload threaded through `BotanikFirmware<In, Out>`, and the PLC-fault watchdog escalation. | active |
| `BOTANIK_FEATURE_NETWORK` | off     | The `network/` stack: @ref Botanik::Network::NetworkHandler + @ref Botanik::Network::NameResolver, instantiated and stepped by `BotanikFirmware`. Requires `PLC` (config comes from the PLC block). | wired; default off (on for `gh_mlb41`) |
| `BOTANIK_FEATURE_NTP`     | off     | @ref Botanik::Network::NtpClient (SNTP). Requires `NETWORK`. | wired; default off |
| `BOTANIK_FEATURE_MQTT`    | off     | @ref Botanik::Network::MqttClient + the alarm notifier. Requires `NETWORK`. | wired; default off |
| `BOTANIK_FEATURE_SUN`     | **on**  | @ref Botanik::Sun::SunPositionTracker + the `solar_elevation()` NOAA math (`sun/`) and its tests. Standalone - not instantiated by `BotanikFirmware`. | active |
| `BOTANIK_FEATURE_CONSOLE` | **on**  | @ref Botanik::Terminal::Console (over the @ref Botanik::Terminal::AnsiTerminal VT device), the built-in shell/logs/alarms pages, the console UART they drive, and the `register_pages()` / `register_commands()` hooks. Off -> a headless firmware. | active |
| `BOTANIK_FEATURE_MONITOR` | **on**  | The secondary MONITOR UART and its @ref Botanik::Logs::SerialLogWriter sink. Independent of `CONSOLE`. | active |

Gate code with the preprocessor `#if BOTANIK_FEATURE_PLC` (it must drop member
declarations and ctor-init entries, which a `constexpr` cannot, and C++11 has no
`if constexpr`). Dependency rules (`NTP`/`MQTT` need `NETWORK`; `NETWORK`
currently needs `PLC`, since its config comes from the PLC block) are enforced by
`#error` guards in the header.

**Building without a PLC.** Configure with `-DBOTANIK_FEATURE_PLC=OFF` and derive
the firmware from `BotanikFirmware<>` (the payload template parameters default to a
zero-size `Empty` type, so a PLC-less site never names a payload). The PLC sync
handler, log reader, and shared region drop out; @ref Botanik::Health::HealthMonitor
runs with a null link context (its POST skips the region check and the watchdog
simply keeps kicking while the loop is alive, so the hardware watchdog still catches
a hang); the Home/Status pages render "n/a" for the absent PLC status.

**Building headless.** Configure with `-DBOTANIK_FEATURE_CONSOLE=OFF` for a
headless firmware. The interactive terminal, the built-in shell/logs/alarms pages,
the console UART they drive, and the `register_pages()` / `register_commands()` hooks
all drop out; the logger, scheduler, health/alarm model, and (if enabled) the PLC link
and network services remain, and diagnostics still reach the MONITOR UART through
@ref Botanik::Logs::SerialLogWriter. The two UARTs are independent: `CONSOLE` and
`MONITOR` gate separately, so a headless build can keep MONITOR for diagnostics and an
interactive build can drop it (`-DBOTANIK_FEATURE_MONITOR=OFF`); with both off, logs
reach only site-registered sinks. A headless site is a thinner subclass that contributes
no pages and overrides only `on_setup()` / `on_loop()`. The built-in terminal sites
(e.g. `gh_mlb41`) keep `CONSOLE` on, since their pages assume the UI.

@section main_concepts Key concepts

- **Nothing blocks.** Every stateful object exposes a `step()` that does a small,
  bounded amount of work and returns immediately; the main loop calls them in a
  fixed order. Blocking would stall the RTOS and trip the watchdog on the OPTA.
- **Config-driven components.** Components that consume operator config (network,
  NTP, MQTT, sun position) derive @ref Botanik::ConfigHandler "ConfigHandler": it
  watches their PLC config block's serial and restarts their state machine when it
  changes. They are also @ref Botanik::Component "Component"s, which log one DEBUG /
  BOOT line on their first `step()` so a component missing from the loop is obvious.
  See @ref md_docs_2PLC__CONFIG "PLC configuration variables and components".
- **HAL-only platform access.** Code never calls `<time.h>` or platform APIs
  directly - only `HAL::Clock::current_ms()`, `HAL::Memory::dsb()`, `HAL::Memory::ordered_copy()`, and
  friends. This is what makes the simulator possible.
- **PLC shared memory.** Host and PLC coprocessor communicate through shared
  memory using transactional read/write patterns and explicit ARM DSB barriers to
  avoid torn reads, with heartbeat / timeout / watchdog tracking.
- **Rate-limited logging.** A global bucket plus a per-topic bucket throttle log
  volume; suppressed-message counts are reported on a slower cadence.
- **Byte-efficient terminal.** The UI targets a low-bandwidth serial link, so the
  console/editor layer minimizes the escape sequences it emits.
- **Self-instrumenting loop.** `PerfMonitor` samples per-tick CPU load, scheduling
  jitter, and stack high-water usage (the last via stack painting), surfaced live
  in the status bar and the `stats` shell command.

The rest of this section:

- @subpage overviews - orientation guides: the concurrency model and the data
  flows through the pages, terminal, and logger.
- @subpage deepdives - mechanism guides: the scheduler/perf instrumentation, the
  PLC shared-memory handshake, and the editor's minimal-byte reconciliation.
- @subpage targets - building and running each target, and porting to new hardware.
- @subpage testing - the test suites and the in-firmware self-tests.
*/
